  "operation": {
    "kind": "prompt",
    "user": "Judge this acceptance suite. One question above all the others: **would these\ntests still pass if the feature delivered nothing useful?**\n\n## The tests\n\n{{.inputs.acceptance_tests}}\n\n## What they reported\n\n{{.inputs.results}}\n\n## The mutation check\n\n{{.inputs.diagnosability}}\n\n## The claims they were supposed to defend\n\n{{.inputs.claims}}\n\n## What phase 5 already covers\n\n{{.inputs.covered}}\n\n## Findings from the previous pass\n\n{{.inputs.prior_findings}}\n\nEach carries the author's own `disposition` — what it says it did:\n\n- `fixed` — check it. A finding recorded as fixed that is not fixed is worse than one\n  never addressed, and it is why this list is here rather than in your memory.\n- `rejected` — the author says the finding was wrong. It may be right. Read the `note`\n  and let it go if it holds; restating a finding already answered is how a loop spends\n  four passes going nowhere.\n- `blocked` — the author cannot see how to make progress. Decide whether that is true.\n  If it is, raise `target: 'ask'` and stop: nothing downstream of you can supply it.\n\nA finding that comes back unchanged twice is a finding about the author state, not the\nwork.\n\n## What to check\n\n- **The central question, per test:** imagine the feature technically working and\n  worthless — the data loads but is wrong, the run starts but never finishes.\n  Does this test still pass? Then it asserts an intermediate, not the success\n  signal. Go through them one at a time; this is the finding worth the whole\n  phase.\n- **Deliverables with no test**, especially the ones that break *silently*: no\n  crash, no error, just wrong. Those need the most coverage and usually have the\n  least.\n- **Tests that take a shortcut the person cannot take** — through the API when\n  the deliverable is reached through the UI. They pass while the real path is\n  broken.\n- **Acceptance tests that re-assert a unit invariant** already covered in phase\n  5: duplicated cost, and a false reading of coverage.\n- **Failure modes invisible from outside.** Every phase-4 mode should be reachable\n  and observable the way the person experiences it, or recorded as unverified.\n- **Diagnosability.** The mutations must make the suite fail *and name the cause*.\n  A suite that goes red without saying why gets ignored in six months. Read the\n  mutation results as evidence, not as a formality.\n- Every `done_when` from phase 5 has a test that would notice it regressing;\n  invalidated tests were deleted or rewritten, not skipped.\n\n## What to produce\n\n`findings` (severity · claim · evidence · target_id), `max_severity`,\n`max_severity_rank` (blocker=3, significant=2, minor=1, note=0), and `target` —\n`draft` to go back to writing tests, `explore` to re-open how the value is\ndriven, `upstream` where the flow or the deliverable is what is wrong.\n\nThere is a fourth `target`, `ask` — neither you nor the author can see how to make\nprogress: what is missing is a measurement nobody has taken, a decision only a person can\nmake, or a fact that is not in the repo. A person always sees this one, and it leaves the\nloop AT ONCE rather than spending the passes that remain. Raise it the pass you know, not\nthe pass you run out — being stuck on pass 1 is not improved by two more passes of the\nsame. The author's `blocked` dispositions are where to look first; judge them rather than\ntaking them at their word.\n",
    "config": {
      "model": "claude-opus-5"
    },
